NAME
    useradd - create a user account (root only)

SYNOPSIS
    useradd <name> [-u <uid>] [-g <group|gid>]

DESCRIPTION
    Adds an account to /etc/passwd and prompts (twice, no echo) for its initial
    password. ROOT ONLY. Creates the home directory /Users/<name> and, on ext2,
    chowns it to the new user.

    The password may not be empty, the same rule passwd enforces. useradd is
    the only writer of an INITIAL secret, so without the check an account made
    by pressing Enter twice would be loginable by pressing Enter. The refusal
    happens before anything is written, so no account is created.

    -u <uid>          use this uid (default: the next free id at/above 1000)
    -g <group|gid>    primary group by name or number (default: a user-private
                      group named after the user, created in /etc/group with
                      gid == uid, the usual convention; if a group of that name
                      already exists, its gid is used instead)

    The password salt comes from the machine's hardware RNG (a virtio-rng
    device, via the RANDOM syscall). Where there is no such device - which is
    most machines; QEMU needs -device virtio-rng-device, and Parallels and the
    Pi have none at all - useradd falls back to a weaker salt derived from the
    monotonic clock and prints "no hardware RNG - using a weaker clock-derived
    salt". That salt is still per-account unique, but an attacker who can bound
    when the account was created can bound it. Account files hold ~20 entries
    (a 2 KB read cap).

    The secret written to /etc/shadow is version 2 (PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-512, about
    a second to derive on the QEMU guest; see login for the line formats). A
    name is at most 32 bytes, all login and the cluster carry.

CLUSTER KEY
    On a node with a cluster realm (/etc/cluster/realm), useradd also derives
    the new user's cluster key from the password (another second) and, once
    the account exists, prints the line for /etc/cluster/users. Put it there
    on every export that should serve this user only with their own key:

        useradd: to require this user's own key at an export, add this line to
          /etc/cluster/users there:
        alice 3f5c...e21a

    An export without the line serves the user on the requesting machine's
    word. With no realm, no line is printed.

ORDER OF OPERATIONS
    useradd writes three things - /etc/group, the home directory, and
    /etc/passwd - and there is no multi-file transaction here. The account line
    is written LAST, so it is the single commit point: if the group or the home
    directory can't be made, nothing is committed and useradd exits non-zero;
    if the account write itself fails, the group entry and home directory it
    just made are removed again. A half-made account is never reported as
    success.

/etc/skel
    If /etc/skel exists, its top-level FILES are copied into a home directory
    this run created - each chowned to the new user, carrying the template
    file's mode. There is no /etc/skel by default; create one to have new
    accounts start with a set of files:

        # mkdir /etc/skel
        # write /etc/skel/README welcome to Ouroboros

    Subdirectories inside /etc/skel are reported and skipped (not copied
    recursively). An existing home directory that useradd merely adopts is
    left alone - skel files only go into a home useradd itself created.

    The copy happens after the account is committed, and a file that fails to
    copy is a WARNING, not an error: the account, its group, and its home are
    already complete without it.

EXAMPLES
    # useradd alice
    New password: ****
    Retype new password: ****
    useradd: deriving the password's hash...
    useradd: deriving the cluster key...
    useradd: created alice
    useradd: to require this user's own key at an export, add this line to
      /etc/cluster/users there:
    alice 3f5c...e21a

    # groupadd devs
    # useradd bob -g devs        put bob in the existing group "devs"

